The MON format is Moth Object Notation as serialised literal data: one root
record written in the shared MON notation and restricted to literal values.
Every value in a completed document must be a supported literal form.

A MON reader never evaluates Moth expressions. Arithmetic, templates, calls,
casts, field projections, variables and constant references are rejected even
when the compiler could fold them. Rejection happens before expression
evaluation, not after evaluating and inspecting a result.

Availability is Rust-only. The implemented Rust caller surface is public as
`moth::mon`, re-exported from `src/lib.rs` over the crate-private
`src/compiler_frontend/mon/` owner. Another Rust crate can execute the service,
but compiled Moth programs have no runtime codec support. Moth-native
operations, automatic schema extraction and the static project builder remain
deferred separately. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

### One root record

A complete document contains exactly one record. Its outer parentheses may be
omitted. These forms have the same record meaning:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    title = "Strategy",
    window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
]

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    (
        title = "Strategy",
        window = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    )
]

A lone scalar, collection or choice is not a document. Put it in a named root
field. The explicit empty root is `()`. Empty and comments-only input denotes
an empty root record. Validation still checks every required field.

Use commas between entries at every level, with one optional trailing comma.
Newlines are whitespace, not a separator. Root fields create no bindings,
scope or references to earlier fields. After an explicit root, only whitespace
and comments may remain. Multiple roots and concatenated documents are
invalid. The file and in-memory document grammars are identical.

### Records, collections and maps

Values nest recursively subject to resource limits and the receiving schema.
Supported values are records, ordered collections, maps, quoted strings,
characters, booleans, numeric literal data, `none`, literal choices and
qualified nominal record data.

Record fields have unique identifier labels and explicit `= value` entries.
They are named-only and carry no annotations, declaration qualifiers, mutable
markers or shorthand initialisers. `()` is an empty record, never a tuple or
unit value.

MON collections may contain different literal kinds and different record
shapes. The receiving schema enforces its element type. Heterogeneous text
changes no Moth collection typing rule and adds no `Any` type to Moth.

Maps preserve insertion order. Keys use the supported Moth map-key families:
`String`, `Bool`, `Char`, `Int`, the fixed-width integers `I8` through `U64`
and `Byte`. Bare names are references, not key shorthand, and are invalid as
MON map keys. The schema checks the declared key and value types. Map entries
and collection items cannot be mixed.

Duplicate keys are rejected rather than keeping the first or last value. Keys
compare as decoded values under the receiving key contract, so different escape
or numeric spellings cannot evade uniqueness. A key of one declared type never
matches another, so `Int`, `I32`, `I64` and `U64` stay distinct key families.
String and Char remain distinct, and Bool is not a numeric key. Record labels
remain identifiers, while map keys are data: a string-keyed map does not become
a record.

The empty forms never change kind:

- `{=}` is always an empty map and `{}` is always an empty collection.
- A map schema rejects `{}` and a collection schema rejects `{=}`. Schema
  context never changes a container's kind.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    scores = {"Priya" = 10, "Rob" = 12},
    empty_scores = {=},
    empty_items = {},
    empty_record = (),
]

### Strings and characters

Strings use double quotes and Char uses single quotes. A Char decodes as
exactly one Unicode scalar value. String contents are preserved without
Unicode normalisation or implicit line-ending conversion. An unescaped newline
or CRLF inside a string stays byte-for-byte content, because MON is data, not
Moth source.

MON strings accept exactly `\\`, `\"`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and `\u{H...}`.
MON characters accept exactly `\\`, `\'`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and `\u{H...}`.
The Unicode braces hold 1 to 6 ASCII hex digits and accept either case on
input; writer output uses uppercase hex. Empty braces, more than six digits,
non-hex digits, surrogate values and out-of-range scalars fail. `\0`, `\xNN`,
fixed-width `\uXXXX`, an escaped physical newline and every other unknown
escape fail.

Templates are producer syntax only and are not accepted string literals in
MON. A folded const template and an evaluated runtime template are each an
ordinary String that a producer quotes; an unevaluated template is not a
serialisable value. Compile time versus runtime concerns the producer and
never changes document validity.

### Numeric data

A numeric literal keeps its lossless spelling until the destination is known.
Whole-number spelling and decimal/exponent spelling remain distinguishable,
and numbers are never routed through `f64` or display formatting.

The lexical rules are strict. Exponents use lowercase `e`; uppercase `E`
fails. Unary `+` fails, and `-` is accepted only when attached to the literal.
`_` is allowed only between decimal digits inside the integer, fraction or
exponent runs. Malformed, edge and double separators fail, and `NaN` and
infinity have no MON spelling.

Conversion is strict about categories:

- `Int`, exact `Integer` and every fixed-width integer from `I8` to `U64`
  require whole-number spelling. Decimal data such as `3.0` fails such a
  target even when mathematically integral, and `1e3` fails it too. `Byte`
  also requires whole-number spelling and accepts only `0` through `255`.
  Formatting cannot rewrite one category as the other.
- Exact `Decimal` data with a declared scale from `0` to `256` accepts whole,
  decimal and exponent forms only when the value is exact after removing
  trailing fractional zeroes and the effective scale is at most the declared
  scale. A scale-two target accepts `1.2` but rejects `1.239` without
  rounding. Exact decimal data is the materialisation path for `Number` and
  `NumberN` schemas, and it rejects inexact values instead of rounding.
- `Float` and the fixed binary floats `F16`, `F32` and `F64` accept all valid
  finite categories and reject non-finite source values and conversion
  results. A fixed binary float materialises with its binary rounding
  contract, so an inexact value rounds instead of failing. Encoding
  round-trips each supported typed value, including `F16`, `F32` and signed
  zero: negative zero writes `-0.0` and positive zero writes `0`.

`Int` and `Float` destinations are profile-dependent, and fixed-width schemas are
profile-independent. The delivered public schema takes no profile input: it
materialises `Int` at `Int32` and `Float` at `Float64` for standalone Rust
callers, and limits a declared decimal scale to `0` through `18`. Schema
preparation capturing the compilation's numeric profile, and the widened `0`
through `256` scale capacity, are accepted end-state design and remain queued.

Large integer and exact decimal data are retained and round-tripped without a
second arithmetic runtime. Unsupported concrete adapters fail explicitly
rather than narrowing or converting through Float. The added widths, `Byte`,
profile capture and the widened scale range are accepted end-state design; see
@../progress (Progress) for current support.

### Structs and choices

Both nested record forms are accepted:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    size = (width = 1280, height = 720),
    size = Size(width = 1280, height = 720),
]

These are alternative examples, not duplicate fields in one document.
Anonymous records use named entries. A qualified nominal record literal
follows the shared constructor argument rules without invoking user code.

A qualifier is one identifier: Unicode alphabetic or underscore start,
Unicode alphanumeric or underscore continuation, case-sensitive. Dotted paths,
aliases, registry lookup and whitespace-separated `::` are invalid. The
receiving schema determines the expected nominal type. An explicit qualifier
is an additional checked assertion, never ignored and never used to select
another schema. Matching payload shapes do not make unrelated nominal types
interchangeable.

Choice data uses Moth-shaped variants rather than an invented tagged-record
mapping:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    widgets = {
        ::Text("Gold"),
        Widget::Progress(value = 0.4),
        ::Ready,
    },
]

A unit variant has no parentheses and a payload variant requires them.
`::Text(...)` uses the expected choice from the receiving schema, while an
explicit `Widget` qualifier must name that same expected choice. An unknown
or mismatching explicit name fails.

Positional arguments precede named arguments. Target slots are routed once,
every required slot is supplied exactly once, and unknown variants, duplicate
payload fields, wrong arity and payload type mismatches fail. Choice payload
fields have no defaults. Typed encoding checks any supplied qualifier before
omitting it, and prefers unqualified named records with named choice payloads.

### Schemas, defaults and options

Schemas define ordinary data shapes, field types, nominal identities, choice
variants and explicit default values. They do not define a second expression
language. Records are closed: every unknown field fails, including a
misspelling of a field whose correct name has a default. Maps provide the
explicit dynamic-key surface.

V1 supplies a small immutable Rust schema description for host callers. The
engine defines its save-data contract once and supplies it explicitly. A
prepared schema and its defaults are validated when prepared, before any
document is processed, and the immutable prepared schema is reused across
calls. Schema misuse is a structured failure, not a panic. There are no
schema files, remote schema loading, runtime schema editing, registries or
predicate DSLs.

Eligibility is derived transitively from the complete concrete schema. Every
field, element, map entry and variant payload must have a supported mapping,
and an unsupported member makes the enclosing shape ineligible even when the
current instance does not use it. Functions, wires, routes, closures, live
handles, unresolved resource pieces and deferred computations have no
ordinary data encoding. A completed runtime-generated String is eligible. No
mandatory serialisation trait, per-type marker, rename or skip attribute,
custom codec hook or constructor execution participates in decoding.

Optionality permits `none`. Only an explicit field default permits omission.
A missing optional field without a default is an error. Missing fields,
explicit `none` and empty values remain distinct.

Defaults are prevalidated data, not runtime factories. A default applies only
at the exact missing field. A supplied `none` is validated as `none` and
never replaced by a default. A supplied nested record is validated as that
record, not deep-merged with the parent's default record.

For a parent field `layout` with no default, omission fails even when every
field inside the Layout schema has a default. Supplying `layout = ()`
permits those inner defaults to apply. If `layout` itself has a default,
omission uses that complete value. `()` remains an empty record at the format
level; it becomes a completed default-valued record only when its receiving
schema permits every omission.

Normal typed encoding emits all completed fields, including explicit `none`
and values equal to defaults.

### Trees, not graphs

The data format represents value trees. Repeated shared source values are
encoded at each occurrence, and decoding establishes no preserved alias
relationship or allocation identity. Public `Value` is an owned tree, so cyclic
host data cannot be represented by this API. Public `SchemaType` values form finite
owned schema trees rather than named schema references. Each schema describes only
its explicit nesting levels, and the codec validates values against those levels
and the receiver's limits. Applications can encode graph links as ordinary
identifiers and reconstruct them explicitly.

### Limits and first errors

The codec consumes complete documents and fails fast. Incremental input,
streams and multiple-document framing remain deferred. Finite configurable
budgets bound input bytes, nesting, node counts (including one additional
node for each map entry), numeric digits, default expansions, decoded bytes
and encoded output bytes. The default depth budget is
64; `Limits::MAX_SAFE_DEPTH` sets a hard ceiling of 64 for configurable
`max_depth`, and schema preparation rejects larger policies. Each counter is checked
before the affected allocation or expansion, including default expansion and
repeated shared-value expansion. The other defaults are 1 MiB of input,
100,000 nodes, 4,096 numeric digits, 16 MiB of decoded bytes, 16 MiB of output
bytes and 10,000 default expansions.

Limits are receiver resource policy, not grammar dialects. A valid document
may exceed a receiver's budget, and that outcome is reported distinctly from
syntax and schema errors.

Both operations publish only complete successful results. On failure, partial
values and output buffers are discarded and no partial public result exists.
Each failure is a structured first error with a stable reason and a field or
element path where available; decode errors include the original input byte
range when available. Messages are rendered only at the caller's diagnostic
boundary.

### Deterministic writing

Typed record fields write in schema declaration order. Collection and map
order is preserved. Public `encode_document` and `encode_value` emit compact
output; pretty formatting is private to writer tests and has no public mode.
Given the same ordered value, schema and encoder version, output is
deterministic, with no promise that all semantically equivalent documents share
identical bytes.

Formatting authored text is distinct from typed encoding. It cannot silently
fill omitted fields, reorder maps, remove unchecked qualifiers or erase
numeric categories.

### Resources and versions

MON carries resource identifiers as ordinary strings. Compiler structural
resource pieces are resolved to their final characters before encoding, and
an unresolved value fails conversion to concrete MON data. V1 performs no
path discovery, URL generation, filesystem checks or resource loading, and no
string scan reconstructs dependencies.

The receiver supplies the schema. There is no mandatory schema header,
reserved universal version field or automatic schema discovery. Applications
may declare an ordinary `format_version` field with explicit migrations.
Closed schemas never silently fall back to another version or repair
mismatches.

### Deferred source and builder surfaces

MON text has no file IO, module imports, schema discovery, command, builder
registration, output write or runtime opcode. Compiler `$mon` convenience,
with its invocation syntax still undecided, belongs to later Moth-native
integration, as do Moth-side encode and decode operations, automatic schema
extraction from ordinary Moth types and the static `.mon` project builder.

MON's local escape decoder adds no source-language syntax. The `{=}` empty-map
distinction, Unicode escapes and contextual `::Variant` construction remain
deferred source parity unless another authorised syntax change has already
delivered them.
